---
1. Flax scope / parameter names
transformer_block_apply now instantiates a new TinyTransformerBlock on every call with the hard-coded name "layer". All blocks therefore share the same path params → layer → …. During training every call to the helper writes back into the same set of weights, so after the first update all N layers collapse into a single shared set of parameters. You have, in effect, a 1-layer network. Repetition such as “Once uponOnce once upon” is a classic symptom.
---
2. Monkey-patched model.apply
Run_training.py replaces model.apply with _apply_jitted, whose signature is (…, rng=None) – but train_step.py still calls model.apply(..., rngs={"dropout": key}). Python lets the call go through (because the extra keyword is silently swallowed by Flax’s internal wrapper), but inside _apply_jitted the dropout key is lost, so dropout is always disabled (deterministic=True from the module’s perspective). No dropout → over-fit-then-collapse.
---
3. Dropout key re-use inside the model
Even when you do pass a key, giant_gpt_apply forwards a single root key to the whole network, then each block draws sub-keys in a deterministic sequence. Because train_step is jitted, that sequence is identical for every batch after the first compile unless you feed a new root key on every step. The old version did; the new one does not (it always forwards rng=None from _apply_jitted). Training therefore becomes almost deterministic.
---
4. Weight-decay mask
The helper builds a fresh GiantGPT inside the jitted function. Optax weight-decay masking is applied to the param tree you hand to the optimiser, not to the tree recreated inside giant_gpt_apply; layers added later with self.scope.put_variable get no decay at all, so they drift and amplify the above problems.
